<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dungan people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dungans</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dungans are Chinese-speaking Muslims; the name is used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> for the Hui people of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. After the failure of the great Muslim revolt against Qing rule of 1862–1877, many fled into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, where their descendants live in Kyrgyzstan and Kazakhstan and speak a Chinese dialect written in Cyrillic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dungan-people.jpg" alt="Dungan people" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Professor Hai Feng, Xinjiang University · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dungan-Girls.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Dungan is a term used in territories of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> for Muslims of Hui origin; Turkic-speaking peoples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> also sometimes call Hui Muslims Dungans. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> the Dungans call themselves Hui. The name is of obscure origin. One theory derives it from the Turkic word doñan, one who turns, comparable to the Chinese hui, and another from Donggan, Eastern Gansu, the region many Dungans trace their ancestry to, though the character gan differs. Central Asian Turkic and Tajik speakers have used it for Chinese-speaking Muslims for several centuries, and English and German writers used it from the 1830s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Dungans of the former Soviet republics descend from Hui who fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> after the Hui Minorities' War, also called the Dungan Rebellion, of the nineteenth century. According to Rimsky-Korsakoff, three groups crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in the severe winter of 1877-1878. About 1,000 people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a>, led by Ma Daren, reached Osh. A second group from Didaozhou in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, led by the ahong Ma Yusuf, settled in the spring of 1878 at Yrdyk near Karakol; they numbered 1,130. A third, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, led by the rebel Bai Yanhu, settled at Karakunuz, now Masanchi, and numbered 3,314.</p><p>Under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> of 1881, which required Russian withdrawal from the Upper Ili basin, the Hui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yining">Kulja</a> area could move to the Russian side; Russian statistics record 4,682 Hui who did so between 1881 and 1883, settling at Sokuluk and in southeastern Kazakhstan and northern Kyrgyzstan. Some Dungans served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. In February 2020 a conflict between ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> and Dungans at Korday in Kazakhstan left ten dead according to official Kazakh sources, and about 600 people fled to Kyrgyzstan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Dungan language, which Dungans call the Hui language, is close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-plain-china">Zhongyuan</a> dialect of Mandarin spoken in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>. It is tonal, preserves archaic Qing-era vocabulary, has loanwords from Russian, Arabic, Persian and Turkic, and is uniquely written in the Cyrillic script. More than two-thirds of Dungans also speak Russian.</p><p>Dungans keep elements of Chinese culture in cuisine and dress, and they practised foot binding until 1948, when the Soviet government banned it. Traditional marriage practices with matchmakers remain widespread. The Shaanxi Dungans hold more closely to Chinese customs than the Gansu Dungans. They have been counted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a> as separate from Chinese, with 36,900 in Kazakhstan in 1999 and 58,409 in Kyrgyzstan in 2009. The nineteenth-century explorer Henry Lansdell noted that they abstained from spirits and opium and worked in farming, gardening and trade.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dungan people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Ili valley, the Taranchis, Uyghur farmers descended from settlers brought by the Dzungars, joined the <b>Dungans</b>, captured the Qing garrisons of Ili, including Huiyuan, and established a sultanate at Ghulja.</blockquote><a href="/state/taranchi-sultanate">Taranchi Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
